First Disease - Measles (Rubeola):
Infectious Disease & Sexual Health
Koplik spots, Cephalocaudal maculopapular rash, Cough/Coryza/Conjunctivitis

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IDS-009
Reason for Visit: High fever, severe relentless cough, and a rapidly spreading rash in an unimmunized patient, requiring urgent assessment for measles pneumonia or encephalitis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and parents
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated an unimmunized young adult with a fever exceeding 40C, photophobia, and respiratory distress. Urgent physical assessment is required to confirm a highly contagious, notifiable disease, isolate the household, and evaluate for acute life-threatening complications (pneumonitis) prior to the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "The light hurts my eyes, my cough won't stop, and I am burning up with this rash everywhere."
History of Present Illness: Illness commenced 4 days ago with a persistent high fever (exceeding 40C), severe systemic malaise, and the classic '3 Cs': a barking, persistent, and severe non-productive cough, coryza with profuse nasal discharge, and non-purulent, distinct severe conjunctivitis. The patient exhibits exquisite photophobia, actively avoiding bright light. Two days ago, the parents noted distinct small white lesions in his mouth. Today, a maculopapular erythematous rash began on the face at the hairline and is actively spreading cephalocaudally down the trunk. Patient has a complete absence of previous childhood MMR vaccination due to parental preference.
Functional Status: Bedbound, highly lethargic.
Social/Safety Concerns: High contagion risk. Unvaccinated siblings present in the household.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 115/70 | HR: 125 | Temp: 40.2 | RR: 24 | SpO2: 95% | Wt: 60 Kg
General: Toxic appearance, marked photophobia (eyes kept shut), continuous harsh coughing, generalized rash.
Physical Exam: Oropharyngeal exam reveals Koplik spots (distinct small white lesions on the erythematous buccal mucosa adjacent to the molars). Eye exam confirms severe bilateral non-purulent conjunctivitis. Chest auscultation reveals widespread harsh breath sounds and scattered rhonchi. Skin exam shows a florid, confluent maculopapular erythematous rash on the face and upper trunk, with discrete lesions on the lower trunk. Generalized distinct tender lymphadenopathy is present. Clinical presentation aligns with the diagnostic tetrad: Koplik spots, Cephalocaudal maculopapular rash, and Cough/Coryza/Conjunctivitis.
Environmental Observations: Room darkened due to photophobia.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: First Disease - Measles (Rubeola) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Rubella - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The severity of the fever (>40C), profound illness toxicity, and the presence of Koplik spots (which are pathognomonic for measles).

Kawasaki Disease - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Age of the patient, presence of severe cough and Koplik spots, and absence of strawberry tongue or peripheral extremity desquamation.

Drug Eruption - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The distinct viral prodrome (3 Cs) and specific cephalocaudal spread of the rash.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Public Health Outbreak Risk)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with classic clinical stigmata of acute measles infection, complicated by high fever and tachycardia. Given the extreme transmissibility of the virus and the high mortality risk associated with potential complications such as secondary bacterial pneumonia or acute encephalitis, immediate on-site clinical evaluation was critical. Deferring this assessment would not only risk the patient's acute respiratory decompensation but also represent a gross failure of public health protocol by delaying immediate isolation and contact tracing. This presentation strictly meets the criteria for urgent after-hours assessment (MBS 585-600).

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribe strict antipyretics and maintain aggressive oral hydration. Mandate immediate and absolute household quarantine. Urgent notification to the State Department of Health Communicable Disease Branch. Request measles-specific IgM antibodies in serum and viral RNA via PCR from a throat/nasopharyngeal swab to be collected under strict airborne precautions.
Positive Behavioral Change: By systematically alternating paracetamol and ibuprofen every 3 hours, the parents will suppress the extreme fever peaks; this will reward the patient with periods of relative comfort and improved oral intake, reinforcing the strict medication schedule.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Public Health Unit follow-up for contact tracing.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Daily telehealth monitoring by GP or Public Health to assess for respiratory compromise or altered mentation.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation (call ahead for airborne isolation) or call 000 if the patient develops increased work of breathing, chest pain, profound lethargy preventing wakening, confusion, or a severe intractable headache.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical but highly probable; conditions are dynamic with a high risk of secondary complications.
Clinical Course: The fever should break approximately 48 hours after the rash appears. The rash will eventually fade to a brown, desquamating stain.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Measles suppresses the immune system severely. You must monitor him constantly. Call an ambulance immediately if he struggles to breathe, seems confused, or cannot be woken up easily.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: There is no specific cure for the virus itself. The immediate goal is preventing life-threatening complications like brain swelling or severe pneumonia while his body fights the infection.
Actionable Steps: Keep the room pitch black to prevent eye pain. Ensure continuous, small sips of fluid. Absolutely no one is to enter or leave this house until Public Health clears you.

SMS INFORMATION

Highly suspected Measles. Strict absolute quarantine at home required. Public Health will contact you. Ensure constant hydration.

SMS EDUCATION

Call 000 immediately if breathing becomes fast/difficult, or if the patient becomes confused or hard to wake up. Give alternating fever medications.
